# per-word timing sidecars: ROLLED OUT 2026-09-17. 1,901 files, 68 MB,
# tracked and published -- they are what makes word-level click-to-seek and
# highlighting work. Pages without one fall back to sentence-level seek via
# the data-t attribute, which is what the site did before and after.

# alignment resume checkpoint: transient, deleted once model.pkl is written
aligned.checkpoint.pkl
*.checkpoint.pkl

# Speaker voice embeddings -- BIOMETRIC DATA, must never be published.
# These are pyannote voiceprints for everyone who spoke at a public meeting,
# including private residents giving public comment. A transcript records what
# someone said in public (the point of this project, and the city publishes the
# video); a voiceprint is a biometric index that supports identifying them
# against unrelated audio. Nobody speaking at a council meeting expects that.
# They were served at https://<site>/<dir>/embeddings.pkl with HTTP 200 until
# 2026-09-17. Keep them locally -- cross-video speaker matching needs them and
# they are expensive to rebuild -- but never in a published tree.
embeddings.pkl

# Auto-accept whitelist: holds contributor bearer tokens, and this repo is
# public. Committing it would publish the exact value that grants the
# privilege. trusted_contributors.example.json is the tracked template.
trusted_contributors.json

# Correction queue: contains submitted free text from the public, pending
# review. Keep triage local until a submission has actually been reviewed.
corrections_queue.json

# Per-transcript, per-speaker wordclouds: 10,295 files, 414 MB, and NOTHING
# links to them. srt2html stopped generating them (the code at srt2html.py:513
# is commented out) and the full regeneration dropped the last references, so
# every one of these is an orphan served to nobody. The electeds/ wordclouds
# are a different thing -- supercut.py still generates and links those, so they
# stay tracked.
20??-??-??_*/*.wordcloud.png

# Provenance artifacts -- preserved in the PRIVATE repo
# medford-transcripts/transcript_backup, never published.
#
# WHY NOT PUBLISHED. model.pkl is the ASR half of a 2-year 24/7 run and the
# .srt variants are its intermediates; none has a reader. The working .srt
# carries SPEAKER_nn labels and NEVER names -- the map lives in
# speaker_ids.json and is resolved only when the HTML is written -- so the
# "raw" file was strictly less useful than the page beside it. The two use
# cases it existed for never materialised: the city's video curator was not
# interested in re-uploading the SRTs, and nobody ever returned a corrected
# speaker_ids.json. The correction form now covers the second at a far lower
# bar.
#
# WHY NOT DELETED. These stay in the working tree; the pipeline reads them
# locally. Untracking only stops PUBLISHING them, and the backup is what makes
# a fresh clone able to rebuild.
#
# PDFs are deliberately NOT here: agendas, minutes and proposals are LINKED
# from the site, and the civicclerk source is of unknown stability.
model.pkl
speaker_ids.json
*.srt
*.srt.orig

# speaker_provenance.json (2026-09-18): per-meeting sidecar recording how each
# speaker_ids.json entry was set (manual / reference_voice / embedding_match /
# propagated / unknown), with score, source cluster key, and previous value
# for rollback (see speaker_provenance.py). Purely derived, never published;
# stays local for the same file-count-drives-Pages-build-time reason as the
# other per-meeting artifacts above (~2,280 more files would undo that win).
speaker_provenance.json

